<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The capability the phone looks for to know whether this watch carries the application.

  Without this file, `CapabilityClient.getCapability("pendulum_watch_app", FILTER_REACHABLE)` finds
  no node **even on a watch where the application is running**, and the phone onboarding concludes
  "application missing" and offers to install it a second time. Three lines of XML for the watch
  half of a detection whose other half was already written.

  The `tools:keep` is **critical** and is not a stylistic precaution: the resource shrinker sees no
  Kotlin reference to this array — it is Google Play Services that reads it, by its name, at
  install time — so it removes it. The symptom then appears only in release, and it is silent:
  detection stops working without any message being produced. `isMinifyEnabled` is `false` today in
  both modules, which keeps the omission invisible until the day it turns `true`.

  The two names are deliberately distinct (`pendulum_watch_app` here, `pendulum_phone_app` on the
  phone side): each side looks for the one opposite, and a shared name would make every device
  capable of detecting itself.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="@array/android_wear_capabilities">
    <string-array name="android_wear_capabilities">
        <item>pendulum_watch_app</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
